<%# locals: (machinery:) %>
<%# THE MACHINERY BEHIND ONE TURN: the state it was played in, and the prompt it
    was written from. Read beside that turn's prose -- the captain's ask of
    2026-09-05 -- so it is built out of what the play page already has (`.sheet`,
    `.sheet-line`, `.sheet-note`) rather than out of the debug page's own
    vocabulary. The reading experience is `ta-api-iface`'s stage and inventing a
    second visual language in an inspector is that stage arriving early.

    The prompt half renders through `debug/_conversation`, the same partial the
    debug page renders, so the two cannot come to disagree about what was sent.

    `.machinery-panel` is the styling scope rather than the `<details>` the play
    page wraps this in, because this same partial is what a browser with scripts
    blocked gets as a page of its own (`MachineryController`) -- there is no
    `<details>` around it there. %>
<div class="machinery-panel">

<%# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    THE STATE, AND IT IS FIVE ROWS. The captain's scope: the story time, who was
    in the room, what was lying in it, what he was carrying, and what each
    person present was carrying. Nothing else -- the branch, the evidence, the
    closed sets, the exits and the cost figures are the debug page's, and a
    panel that sits beside the prose has to be readable at a glance.

    EVERY ROW SAYS WHICH KIND OF FACT IT IS. Two of the five are columns written
    when the turn was played and are therefore AS OF THAT TURN; the other three
    are `Item` rows, and an item row says where a thing is NOW. Nothing in the
    app records what was lying on a floor at a past story moment, so a panel
    claiming to would be inventing one. See `Playthrough::Machinery`.
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
<div class="sheet">
  <h2>the state this turn was played in</h2>

  <div class="sheet-line">
    <span class="who">story time</span>
    <span class="state"><%= debug_time(machinery.story_time, absent: "not recorded") %><span class="mark">this turn</span></span>
  </div>

  <div class="sheet-line">
    <span class="who">in the room</span>
    <span class="state"><%= debug_value(machinery.cast.map(&:fullname).to_sentence.presence,
                                        absent: "nobody recorded — a turn played before Playthrough::Turn#play snapshotted a cast") %><span class="mark">this turn</span></span>
  </div>

  <div class="sheet-line">
    <span class="who">on the floor</span>
    <span class="state"><%= debug_value(machinery.lying_here.map(&:name).to_sentence.presence,
                                        absent: "nothing to pick up") %><span class="mark">now</span></span>
  </div>

  <div class="sheet-line">
    <span class="who">you carry</span>
    <span class="state"><%= debug_value(machinery.carried.map(&:name).to_sentence.presence,
                                        absent: "carrying nothing") %><span class="mark">now</span></span>
  </div>

  <%# WHAT EACH PERSON IN THE ROOM IS CARRYING, and it is ONE row rather than one
      per person: five short rows is the whole shape of this half, and a row that
      multiplies with the cast would push the prompt half off the screen in a
      crowded room. The protagonist is not in it -- the party's own hands are the
      row above, and a party's copies are in neither a room nor anybody's hands
      (`Item`). %>
  <div class="sheet-line">
    <span class="who">they carry</span>
    <span class="state"><%= debug_value(machinery.held.map { |holding|
                                          "#{holding.character.fullname}: " +
                                            (holding.items.map(&:name).to_sentence.presence || "nothing")
                                        }.to_sentence.presence,
                                        absent: "nobody else was in the room") %><span class="mark">now</span></span>
  </div>

  <p class="sheet-note">
    The story time and the cast are columns written when the turn was played, so
    they are as of this turn. The three item rows are read as they stand now:
    nothing records what was lying in <%= machinery.location&.name || "a room" %>,
    or in whose hands, at a past story moment.
  </p>
</div>

<%# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    THE PROMPT, AND WHAT CAME BACK. The prose call first and the classifier
    second, which is the opposite of the order the loop made them in and the
    right order to READ them in: the passage above is being compared against the
    prompt that produced it, and how the line was classified is context for that
    rather than the thing being compared.
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
<div class="sheet">
  <h2>what the narrator was given</h2>

  <% if machinery.prose_conversations.any? %>
    <%= render partial: "debug/conversation", collection: machinery.prose_conversations, as: :exchange %>
  <% else %>
    <p class="sheet-note">
      <%= debug_value(nil, absent: machinery.opening? ?
            "nothing — the opening arrival was generated when the world was built, not on a turn anybody played" :
            no_receipts_reason) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# PART OF WHAT THE MODEL WAS GIVEN, and its own heading. A turn the grammar
      resolved (`scenes.resolved_by`) made no classifier call at all, which is
      the point of the slash menu and is worth reading as an absence rather than
      as a gap. %>
  <h2>what the classifier was given</h2>
  <% if machinery.classifier_conversations.any? %>
    <%= render partial: "debug/conversation", collection: machinery.classifier_conversations, as: :exchange %>
  <% else %>
    <p class="sheet-note">
      <%= debug_value(nil, absent: "no classifier call on this turn — Playthrough::Grammar read the line, or nothing did") %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>
</div>
